## Flow Diagram

```
┌──────────────────────────────────────┐
│           SERVER FLOW                │
├──────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                      │
│   1. socket()    → Create socket     │
│         │                            │
│   2. setsockopt()→ Set options       │
│         │                            │
│   3. bind()      → Bind to port      │
│         │                            │
│   4. listen()    → Start listening   │
│         │                            │
│   5. accept()  ◄─── Client connects  │
│         │                            │
│   6. recv()/send() ↔ Communicate     │
│         │                            │
│   7. close()     → Close connection  │
│         │                            │
│      Loop back to step 5             │
└──────────────────────────────────────┘
```

## Key Points

| Function | Purpose |
|----------|---------|
| `socket()` | Creates an endpoint for communication |
| `setsockopt()` | Sets socket options (e.g., reuse address) |
| `bind()` | Assigns an address/port to the socket |
| `listen()` | Marks socket as passive (accepting connections) |
| `accept()` | Accepts an incoming connection |
| `recv()` / `send()` | Receives/sends data on the connection |
| `close()` | Closes the socket descriptor |

> **Note**: The basic server handles one client at a time. The multi-client version uses `fork()` to handle multiple clients simultaneously. For production use, consider using `select()`, `poll()`, `epoll()`, or threading (`pthread`) for better scalability.

